<CaseStudy
  title="Sargfabrik"
  subtitle="From coffin factory to rather more life."
  heroSrc="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Wien-Penzing%20-%20Wohn-%20und%20Kulturprojekt%20Sargfabrik%20-%2010%20-%20Dachgarten.jpg?width=1400"
  heroPlaceholder="Sargfabrik hero"
  heroCredit="Haeferl · CC BY-SA 3.0"
  heroCreditHref="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wien-Penzing_-_Wohn-_und_Kulturprojekt_Sargfabrik_-_10_-_Dachgarten.jpg"
  facts={facts}
  gallery={gallery}
  prev={{ href: '/r50/', label: 'R50' }}
  next={{ href: '/brutopia/', label: 'Brutopia' }}
>
  <section class="case-reading mx-auto max-w-[820px] px-6 pt-12 sm:px-12">
    <p>The name really does mean coffin factory. A resident association bought the Vienna site in 1989; Sargfabrik opened with 73 homes in 1996. Miss Sargfabrik added 39 homes in 2000. The often-quoted 112-home total combines both projects, rather than describing one building completed in 1996. <a class="source-link" href="https://sargfabrik.at/wohnen/architektur">[Project source]</a></p>
    <p>Housing sits alongside cultural and shared facilities, including a bathhouse, event spaces and a roof garden. The association’s chronology also records planning revisions and objections before construction. This was sustained organizing and redevelopment, not a cheap factory waiting to become an instant utopia.</p>
    <h2>Give the neighborhood a reason to visit.</h2>
    <p>A concert, a meal or a swim can make a residential project part of a wider neighborhood. Those activities also bring staffing, insurance, sound separation, ticketing and maintenance. The exciting question is not simply “could we have baths?” It is “who can run them well, year after year?”</p>
    <p>The industrial history is worth keeping, but a former factory is not automatically suitable for housing. Survey structure, contamination, daylight and existing livelihoods before choosing what to preserve, replace or add.</p>
    <aside class="research-note mt-8">Reading lens: project publications document the place and its intended design. They are not independent evaluations of affordability, energy performance or how every resident experiences life there. The questions here are our interpretation.</aside>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="lessons">
<li>Keep the two projects and their dates distinct when comparing scale.</li>
<li>Pair public facilities with capable operators; goodwill cannot replace an operating budget.</li>
<li>Treat long-running self-management as experience to learn from, not proof that conflict or maintenance are solved.</li>
</Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="sources"><a href="https://sargfabrik.at/wohnen/architektur">Sargfabrik · architecture and project chronology</a>. Reviewed October 2026. Approximate counts are identified; population changes over time.</Fragment>
</CaseStudy>
